    "summary": "High‑energy Nassau ’73 with soaring Eyes and epic Weather Report Suite",
    "blurb": "The set bursts open with a powerful Weather Report Suite that showcases Keith's inventive organ work. Highlights include Jerry’s soaring solo on Eyes Of The World, the classic China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue, and an unforgettable He's Gone. Phil’s thunderous bass and Billy’s tight groove keep the momentum high throughout.",
    "review": "September 8, 1973 at Nassau Coliseum finds the Dead firing on all cylinders. From the opening “Bertha” through a relaxed first set that builds into a towering Weather Report Suite (Prelude, Part 1, then Let It Grow), the band feels both cohesive and adventurous. Jerry’s lead work reaches new heights on Eyes Of The World, delivering what many listeners describe as his \"best ever\" solo, while Phil locks in with Bob for a deep, mind‑synched groove that drives the jams forward. Keith adds a Hancock‑style organ passage to the suite and even lends his voice on several numbers, enriching the texture without ever overwhelming the mix. Billy’s steady drumming provides the perfect foundation for the high‑energy second set, where China Cat Sunflower seamlessly segues into I Know You Rider and He's Gone erupts with raw intensity before the band rockets through Truckin' and a double Not Fade Away. The encore closes on a soulful Stella Blue that gently eases the audience back home. Across three source recordings – two pristine soundboards and a warm matrix – the performance shines, making this show a standout example of the 1973 era.",
